cards.answer:
Default parameters let you initialize a function argument with a fallback value when it is `undefined` (or omitted). The default expression is evaluated **at call time**, not at definition time. Example:
```js
function greet(name = 'World') {
  return `Hello, ${name}!`;
}
greet();        // 'Hello, World!'
greet('Alice'); // 'Hello, Alice!'
```

JavaScript closures hold a live reference to the variable binding in the enclosing scope, not a snapshot of its value at creation time. This means if the variable changes after the closure is created, the closure sees the updated value. For example:
```js
let x = 1;
const get = () => x;
x = 2;
console.log(get()); // 2
```

A rest parameter (`...args`) collects all remaining arguments into a **real Array**. Unlike `arguments`, rest parameters: (1) are a true `Array` with all array methods, (2) only capture arguments not explicitly named, and (3) are available inside arrow functions (which have no `arguments` of their own). Note that using any rest/default/destructured parameter makes the function's parameter list a strict-mode context, so its `arguments` object no longer aliases (tracks changes to) the named parameters. Example:
```js
function sum(first, ...rest) {
  return rest.reduce((a, b) => a + b, first);
}
```

When closures are created inside a `var`-based loop, all of them share the same single `var` binding. By the time any callback fires, the loop has finished and the shared variable holds its final value. Example:
```js
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
// prints: 3, 3, 3
```
Fix it with `let` (block-scoped, one binding per iteration) or an IIFE.

